<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<manifest>
  <!--
    The version here is what the Power Platform upgrades on, and the release
    workflow refuses to publish a tag that disagrees with it. Bump this in the
    same commit as the tag.
  -->
  <control namespace="__NAMESPACE__"
           constructor="__CONTROL__"
           version="0.1.0"
           display-name-key="__CONTROL___Name"
           description-key="__CONTROL___Desc"
           control-type="virtual">

    <!--
      Leave this disabled unless the control calls a third-party service.

      `enabled="true"` makes the control **premium**, and premium is a cost
      somebody else pays: every end user of an app containing this component
      then needs a Power Apps licence rather than an Office 365 one. Nothing
      fails to build, nothing warns at install, and the person who finds out is
      not the person who typed it — so this is worth confirming with whoever
      owns the control rather than flipping to make a `fetch` work.

      When it is genuinely enabled, list every domain. `<domain>` children are
      declarative and can be listed under `enabled="false"` too, which is the
      schema's own second example; it is the `enabled` attribute alone that
      carries the licensing consequence.

          <external-service-usage enabled="true">
            <domain>tile.example.com</domain>
          </external-service-usage>

      `npm run check` fails on `enabled="true"` with no domain, and warns on
      `enabled="true"` at all.
    -->
    <external-service-usage enabled="false" />

    <!--
      The control's only property, and it is not a setting.

      A grid customizer is configured by being *named* on a grid, not by having
      values set on it. The Power Apps grid generates an event name, passes it
      in through this property, and listens on it; the control fires its cell
      renderers and editors back through `context.factory.fireEvent(eventName,
      …)`. Nothing else about this control is configurable, and an empty value
      here means it does nothing at all — the platform is what fills it in.

      Bound rather than input because that is what the template Microsoft
      publishes declares, and the grid binds it rather than passing a static
      string. It is also why index.ts fires from `updateView` as well as
      `init`: a bound property is not guaranteed to carry its value on the
      first pass.

      Do not add maker-facing input properties here expecting to configure the
      overrides. They would work, but a customizer is assigned to a table's
      whole grid, so any value set on it applies to every column of that type
      on every view of that table — which is almost never what the setting was
      meant to say. Narrow inside the override instead, off
      `colDefs[columnIndex].name`.

      KEEP THE NAME `EventName`. In the platform the host generates the value,
      but nothing does in PCFHub's demo harness, so the harness seeds it — and
      it finds the property by looking for one called `EventName`, falling back
      to the single text input a customizer manifest has. Rename this and give
      the control a second text property and the harness stops finding it: the
      demo loads cleanly, `init` returns at its own guard, and nothing on the
      page says why.
    -->
    <property name="EventName"
              display-name-key="EventName_Name"
              description-key="EventName_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="bound"
              required="true" />

    <resources>
      <code path="index.ts" order="1" />
      <css path="css/__CONTROL__.css" order="1" />
      <!--
        React 16.14.0 and Fluent 8, matching the grid customizer template
        Microsoft publishes. Note the 8 — the rest of this repository's React
        variants are on Fluent 9, and this one is deliberately not.

        Not a nostalgic choice: the elements these overrides return are rendered
        by whichever React instance the *host* owns, so the hooks inside them
        have to dispatch through that same instance — which is what declaring
        them as platform libraries guarantees. Fluent 8 in particular needs no
        provider above it, and a cell renderer has nowhere to put one: the grid
        mounts each returned element on its own, inside a cell.
      -->
      <platform-library name="React" version="16.14.0" />
      <platform-library name="Fluent" version="8.121.1" />
      <!--
        Every language the control ships.

        A .resx that exists on disk but is not listed here is not shipped, and
        nothing warns — the file is simply never packed, and the control runs
        in English for that locale while the repository looks translated.
        `npm run check` catches both that and a key missing from a language.

        Drop the ones you will not maintain. A stale translation is worse than
        an honest fallback to English, and every key added to 1033 is a key
        owed to each file listed here.

        THE ORDER IS DELIBERATE AND 1033 GOES LAST. `pcf-start` picks the .resx
        by manifest order rather than by locale: it reads whichever file is
        listed *last* and ignores the LCID and the browser language entirely.
        The platform itself ignores the order completely — a real app resolves
        from the user's provisioned language — so `npm start` is the only thing
        in the world that reads this order, and it is ordered for it. Listing
        1033 first is the tidier-looking arrangement and it makes `npm start`
        render the whole control in Japanese, which reads as a broken build.

        The consequence to remember: ADD A NEW LANGUAGE ABOVE THIS LINE, not
        below it. Appending a sixth file after 1033 silently moves the harness
        to that language, and the symptom shows up in a `npm start` session
        nobody connects to a manifest edit.
      -->
      <resx path="strings/__CONTROL__.3082.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/__CONTROL__.1036.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/__CONTROL__.1031.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/__CONTROL__.1041.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/__CONTROL__.1033.resx" version="1.0.0" />
    </resources>
  </control>
</manifest>
